{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361402,"nonce":"4uKNFaVnkW0mTC6EFvBKsFBqOqg=","title":"The async executor re-polls a task only through its own timers","message":"`drive_until` (crates/zyntax_embed/src/runtime/promise.rs) advances a task in two ways: its first poll, and `host_futures::drive_next_timer_with_task`, which resolves the nearest timer's parked future and, when that future's state machine reaches Ready, marks `promises[task_id]` Ready with that value. A pending task with no timer of its own is re-polled only when no timer is left anywhere. Two consequences follow from the code; both were found by reading and have not been reproduced:\n\n1. A nested await completes the root task with the inner function's value. With `async def inner(): i64 { await sleep(10) return 5 }` and `async def outer(): i64 { let x = await inner() return x + 1 }`, the parked future is `inner`'s state machine, so resolving it reports 5 as `outer`'s result, and the rest of `outer` (the `+ 1`, its handler pops, its fiber drops) never runs. Expected: 6.\n2. A task that returns Pending without parking a timer (an await whose inner promise was already Ready, since `lower_await_calls`' ready block saves the result and returns Pending; a non-resumable perform inside an async function) waits until every other task's timers have fired.\n\nThe Python async design on e9086df7a988adbd490fd072b65be7ffa70295a5b4aaecc653b39fd078d4ce51 calls for the same pieces (a wake that re-polls the root task, the ready-block fall-through).\n\nDone: resolving a leaf future wakes the task that owns it, which re-polls from its root state machine; a Pending return with nothing parked re-queues the task at once; the program above returns 6, and a task behind an already-ready await does not wait on another task's timer.","files":null}]}